# zenv: project environments on laptops and HPC clusters

*I first wrote this post in October 2026 for zenv v0.28 and updated it in November 2026 for v0.34. The new parts are environments in Apptainer and Docker images, packages that modules provide being reused even when a dependency pulls them in, `zenv remove`, dependency groups, uv picked automatically, and bytecode kept in one directory.*

[Mean time per command on an Apple Silicon Mac and an HPC system, as hyperfine means with zenv v0.28.1. The HPC system is a shared JURECA login node with 256 CPUs, and the project is on GPFS.](<$image.asset('fig-speed.svg').attrs('full').alt('Horizontal bar chart of mean times on an Apple Silicon Mac and an HPC system.')>)

Before a Python project runs on JURECA, one of the clusters at the Jülich
Supercomputing Centre, the shell needs three things: the site's modules for the
compiler stack including Python, a virtual environment built on that Python, and the
variables the project reads. By hand, that is

```bash
module load Stages/2026 StdEnv Python
source ~/sim/.venv/bin/activate
export SIM_DATA=/p/scratch/myproject/sim/data
```

typed again in every new shell and copied into every batch script(+ a one time cost of setting up the environment).
The order matters as well: the venv's Python is the module's Python, and without the modules loaded it does not start at all, because it
cannot find its own `libpython3.13.so`. And the steps are slow. On a JURECA
login node, loading the modules and starting Python takes about a second, of
which Python itself needs **20 ms**.

[zenv](https://github.com/anoopkcn/zenv) is a tool I wrote to keep these steps in a file next to the code, so that a
project's environment can be rebuilt on any machine and entered by name. It
covers the cluster side, the modules and their slow loading, as well as the
Python side, and every part is optional: on a laptop, the same tool can manage
nothing but a venv.

It treats the environment as data. zenv records what activating an environment does, and replays that record in subsequent activations. The result is fast,
reproducible, and usable from any shell, directory, batch script and Jupyter kernel.

It also comes with a shell hook that activates the environment when you `cd` into the project and deactivates it when you leave the directory. No variable pollution, no stray entries in `PATH`, and no typing the same three lines over and over.

## [One file next to the code]($heading.id('one-file-next-to-the-code'))

The three lines become an entry in `zenv.json` at the root of the project. A
file can hold several environments, here one for a laptop and one for JURECA:

```json
{
  // one environment for any machine, one for JURECA
  "sim": {
    "target_machines": ["*"],
    "dependency_file": "requirements.txt",
    "env": { "SIM_DATA": "${ZENV_PROJECT_DIR}/data" }
  },
  "sim-jureca": {
    "target_machines": ["jurecadc"],
    "modules": ["Stages/2026", "StdEnv", "Python"],
    "dependency_file": "requirements.txt",
    "env": { "SIM_DATA": "/p/scratch/myproject/sim/data" }
  }
}
```

`target_machines` names the machines an environment is meant for, and zenv matches it against `$SYSTEMNAME` where a site sets it. So one file can describe several environments for different machines. zenv builds the environments in `.zenv` next to `zenv.json`, or in the directory that a top-level `"base_dir"` key names. It expands variables like `$HOME`, `${ZENV_PROJECT_DIR}` and `${ZENV_ENV_DIR}` and takes everything else literally. The file may contain `//` and `/* */` comments, which plain JSON does not allow.

Build the environment once on each machine, and use it from then on:

```bash
zenv setup sim-jureca                 # modules, venv, requirements.txt
source $(zenv activate sim-jureca)    # activate; deactivate undoes it
zenv run sim-jureca python train.py   # one command, no activation
```


Setup installs the packages with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) when uv is on `PATH` and with pip otherwise. `--uv` makes uv required, and `--pip` uses pip even when uv is there.

Setup also registers the environment in `~/.zenv/registry.json`, which lets the other commands find
it by name from any directory. Inside the project, `.` stands for the project's
environment on the current machine, so `zenv run . python train.py` works
unchanged on the laptop and on JURECA. 

For a new project, `zenv init` writes a `zenv.json` from what it finds, such as a `requirements.txt` or a `pyproject.toml`, and `zenv setup <name> --init` writes the file and builds the environment in one step. `zenv validate` reports mistakes in the file with line numbers.

## [Activation as data]($heading.id('activation-as-data'))

An activation script is a small program. Each time it runs, it calls `module
load`, which has Lmod work out the modules again, and then edits `PATH` and the
other variables. zenv runs those steps once, during setup, and keeps the result.

To do that, setup starts one bash process that purges the modules, writes out
its environment with `env -0`, loads the modules, and writes the environment out
again. The difference between the two snapshots is the module cache: the
variables the modules set, and the entries they put in front of or behind the
old value of `PATH`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` and the other path variables. zenv stores
it as a list of operations (set, prepend, append, unset), followed by the
operations for the rest of activation: the venv's `VIRTUAL_ENV` and `bin/`
directory, the variables from `env`, and a few for zenv's own use. Together they
make up `zenv-env.json`, the record of what activating the environment does.

Every way of entering the environment reads that record. The activation scripts
for bash and zsh, csh and tcsh, and fish are generated from it, and `zenv run`
applies it to its own process without starting a shell.

[zenv setup gathers what activation does into one record, which the activation scripts and zenv run then read.](<$image.asset('fig-env-as-data.svg').attrs('full').alt('Three inputs (module load, the venv, env in zenv.json) feed zenv-env.json, which is read by activate.sh, activate.csh, activate.fish and zenv run')>)

Replaying the record costs little. On a JURECA login node, `zenv run` starts
Python from a module-based environment in 21 ms, where Python started directly
takes about 20 ms. With `"module_cache": false`, zenv calls Lmod on every use
instead, and the same command takes about a second. Activation in an
interactive shell gains in the same way, since with the cache the generated
script does not call `module`: in bash it takes 9 ms with the cache and about a
second without.

Because zenv knows exactly what activation changed, it can undo it.
`deactivate` gives each variable its previous value back, or unsets it if it had
none, and activating a second environment deactivates the first. Switching back
and forth leaves no stray entries in `PATH`.

The record also makes the environment usable where nobody types `source`. `zenv
run` replaces itself with the command it starts, so the exit status and signals
are the command's own, which is what a batch script expects from `zenv run
sim-jureca python train.py`. Jupyter kernels made with `zenv jupyter create
sim-jureca` start through `zenv run` as well. A notebook then gets the
environment's modules even when the Jupyter server was started with other ones,
and the Interrupt button reaches the Python process. The environment needs
`ipykernel` for this.

Two things cannot be recorded. Hooks in `activate.commands` or
`activate.script` are shell code, so an environment that has them is run by
sourcing its `activate.sh` in bash; plain variables belong in `env`, where they
stay data. And the module cache does not notice when the site changes its
modules. It is tied to the cluster through `$SYSTEMNAME`, so it is reused on
every node of JURECA and never replayed on another system, but after a module is
updated during maintenance, run `zenv setup` again to capture it afresh.

## [Python on top of the modules]($heading.id('python-on-top-of-the-modules'))

On a laptop the Python part is most of what zenv does. On a cluster it has to
fit around the modules.

zenv picks the interpreter in a fixed order: a Python from the loaded modules
first, then the `fallback_python` named in `zenv.json`, then a Python installed
and pinned with `zenv python install` and `zenv python pin`, then the system's `python3`. `zenv python install` builds CPython from source and passes the options after `--` on to its `./configure`, and `zenv python unpin` removes the pin. If the `pyproject.toml` that setup reads, as the dependency file or with `-e` (`--editable`, which installs the project itself), declares `requires-python`, setup checks the chosen interpreter against it before creating anything, and when it does not match, says which of these to change.

zenv writes the venv itself. A venv is a `pyvenv.cfg` file, `bin/python` links
to the base interpreter and empty `site-packages` directories, and zenv creates
them directly, without `python -m venv` or `ensurepip`. It then installs pip into the venv, with uv when setup installs with uv. A new environment without packages is ready in 115 ms on a Mac, where `python -m venv` alone takes 1.5 s.

Modules often ship Python packages of their own, such as numpy, mpi4py or PyTorch built against the site's libraries. Before installing, zenv reads the package metadata in the directories on the modules' `PYTHONPATH`, the same `*.dist-info` and `*.egg-info` entries that `importlib.metadata` reads, so it knows which packages the modules provide and in which versions.

Leaving those packages out of the requirements is not enough, because a dependency can pull one in again. uv does not look at `PYTHONPATH`, so it would install a second torch into the venv for a requirement like `sentence-transformers`, and the module's copy would shadow it at runtime anyway. zenv therefore has uv resolve the requirements with the module packages pinned to the versions the modules ship, drops those packages from the result, and installs only the rest. A requirement that needs another version of a module package fails setup with uv's explanation of the conflict, instead of installing a copy that never gets imported. On JURECA, with the PyTorch module and `sentence-transformers` as the only requirement, zenv reused 13 packages from the modules and installed 17. The venv holds 5,406 files and directories, where v0.30 installed torch into it and made 29,749, which counts against the file quota on a cluster. With pip, zenv leaves out the requirements that a module provides and lists the ones whose version does not match. `--force` installs everything into the venv.

New dependencies go in with `zenv add`, which installs a package and then records it in the project's manifest, and `zenv remove` undoes it:

```bash
zenv add . "xarray>=2024.1"     # recorded in the manifest
zenv add . pytest --dev         # as a development dependency
zenv add . sphinx --group docs  # in a PEP 735 dependency group
zenv remove . pytest --dev      # out of the manifest and the venv
```

Where the package goes depends on what the environment uses: a line appended to `requirements.txt`, an entry in `[project].dependencies` (or `[dependency-groups].dev` with `--dev`, and the named group with `--group`) of `pyproject.toml`, where zenv rewrites only that entry and leaves comments alone, or the `dependencies` list in `zenv.json`. The manifest changes only after the install succeeds, so a misspelled package name leaves the files as they were. A group recorded this way is installed again on every rebuild, and `zenv setup --group docs` installs an existing group. `zenv remove` takes the same flags, so that it looks where `zenv add` wrote. It leaves a package installed while another installed package still requires it.

Python writes compiled bytecode next to every module it imports, which on a cluster means thousands of small files in the venv. `zenv init` therefore writes two `env` entries into a new Python environment: `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` set to `""`, so Python keeps its bytecode and starts faster the next time, and `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX`, which collects it in one `pycache` directory under the base directory, or under `$ZENV_PYCACHE_DIR` when that is set, for example on a scratch filesystem. Both are plain entries that you can change or delete. `zenv clean` removes the bytecode an environment has left behind, and `--project` also removes the `__pycache__` directories in the project's own sources.

## [From any directory]($heading.id('from-any-directory'))

Registered environments can be used by name wherever you are. `zenv ls` lists
the ones for the current machine, `cd $(zenv cd sim-jureca)` goes to the
project, and `zenv alias create` gives an environment a second name.

Aliases also help on shared filesystems. JURECA and JUWELS mount the same
project directories, so one checkout serves both, with an environment for each
machine in its `zenv.json`. Give both environments the same alias, and the alias
picks the one whose `target_machines` match the machine you are on:

```bash
zenv alias create hpc sim-jureca
zenv alias create hpc sim-juwels
zenv run hpc python train.py    # sim-jureca on JURECA, sim-juwels on JUWELS
```

`.` resolves the same way.

For zsh and bash there is a hook that activates a project's environment when you
`cd` into the project and deactivates it when you leave, much as direnv does:

```bash
eval "$(zenv hook zsh)"    # in ~/.zshrc; for ~/.bashrc, zenv hook bash
```

It activates only environments you have set up yourself, so the `zenv.json` in a
freshly cloned repository does nothing until you run `zenv setup`.

zenv also keeps an environment in step with its configuration. `zenv run`,
`zenv activate` and `zenv cd` compare the environment's entry in `zenv.json`,
and the dependency and module files it names, with their state at the last
setup. If anything changed, they rebuild first, with the flags of that setup, so `--pip` and `--group` stick. `zenv jupyter create` checks the same way. Add a line to `requirements.txt`, and the next `zenv run` installs the package before it runs your command. `ZENV_NO_AUTO_SETUP=1` turns the rebuilds off. When the module list has not
changed, the rebuild replays the module cache instead of calling Lmod again; on
JURECA that brought the setup of a three-module environment from 1.25 s down to
0.16 s. The `cd` hook is the exception. It never rebuilds, so that `cd` stays
fast, and prints a line saying the environment is out of date instead.

## [Environments without Python]($heading.id('environments-without-python'))

Nothing above requires Python. A C++ project can use zenv for its compilers and
variables alone:

```json
{
  "base_dir": ".zenv",
  "tools": {
    "target_machines": ["jurecadc"],
    "python": false,
    "modules": ["Stages/2026", "GCC", "CMake"],
    "env": {
      "PATH": { "prepend": "${ZENV_PROJECT_DIR}/scripts" },
      "OMP_NUM_THREADS": "8"
    }
  }
}
```

Setup loads and caches the modules, creates an empty `bin/` directory that
activation puts on `PATH`, and runs any setup hooks. There is no venv, and
activation does not set `VIRTUAL_ENV`, so uv and pip will not mistake the
environment for one. Setting `"python": true` later adds a venv in the same
directory and keeps the modules, the hooks and the files in `bin/`.

## [Environments in containers]($heading.id('environments-in-containers'))

Some software comes as a container image, such as a PyTorch or vLLM image built for a cluster's GPUs. An environment with a `container` key runs in an [Apptainer](https://apptainer.org) image, or in a [Docker](https://www.docker.com) image with `"runtime": "docker"`:

```json
{
  "train": {
    "target_machines": ["jupiter"],
    "container": { "image": "containers/vllm.sif", "options": ["--nv"] },
    "dependency_file": "pyproject.toml"
  }
}
```

Setup builds the venv on the image's Python and treats the image's packages as it treats a module's: they are resolved against and not installed again. On JUPITER, with a vLLM image and `peft` as the only requirement, zenv reused ten packages from the image, among them torch and transformers, and installed only `peft`. `zenv run train python finetune.py` then starts the command in the image, with the project, the environment and the current directory bound at the same paths.

zenv stays on the host and only starts commands in the image, so the image needs `sh`, `env` and, for a Python environment, `python3` with pip, but nothing from zenv. A container environment cannot be activated in your shell, because the shell runs outside the image; `zenv run train bash` gives you a shell inside it. When the image file changes, the environment is rebuilt on its next use, as it is when `zenv.json` changes.

## [How fast it is]($heading.id('how-fast-it-is'))

zenv is a single binary written in Zig, statically linked on Linux, and needs no
Python to run, so it works on a machine before any Python is set up. Its own
commands cost about as much as starting an empty process. The time you notice
comes from the programs it starts: uv, Lmod and Python.

| Mean time (ms) | Mac | HPC system |
|---|---:|---:|
| `true`, an empty process | 1.2 | 2.1 |
| `zenv ls` | 1.9 | 2.0 |
| `zenv activate <env>` | 2.0 | 2.2 |
| `zenv run <env> true` | 3.1 | 3.9 |
| `bash -l`, a login shell alone | – | 495 |
| `source $(zenv activate <env>)` in a login shell, with modules | – | 525 |
| `module load` and `source .venv/bin/activate` by hand, in a login shell | – | 1,586 |
| `zenv setup <env> --uv`, nothing changed | 21 | 166 |
| `zenv setup <env> --uv`, new environment without packages | 115 | 358 |
| `python -m venv`, for comparison | 1,456 | 2,618 |

The numbers are hyperfine means for zenv 0.28 on an Apple Silicon Mac and on an
HPC system, a JURECA login node (256 CPUs, project on GPFS). The login node is
shared with other users, so its numbers vary by about 0.8 ms. The three
login-shell rows need modules, so the Mac has none; they come from an earlier
run with zenv 0.27.8, whose `zenv activate` took 1.9 ms longer. I have not measured v0.34 again.

## [Getting it]($heading.id('getting-it'))

zenv runs on Linux and macOS, on x86_64 and aarch64:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anoopkcn/zenv/HEAD/install.sh | bash
```

Run the same command again to update. `zenv help` prints the full reference, with every command and every key of `zenv.json`, and `zenv help <command>` prints the part for one command. The source is at [github.com/anoopkcn/zenv](https://github.com/anoopkcn/zenv).
